<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The owner's cat, lifted off the beige plate it was drawn on and put back on one deliberately.

  The plate is not a choice between having one and not having one - measured on API 37, the launcher
  always draws one. A transparent adaptive background renders as a BLACK disc, and a legacy PNG with
  alpha renders as a WHITE disc with the artwork shrunk inside it. So the only question is which
  colour, and beige is the colour the owner's own artwork already used.

  The launcher also draws a ring outside the plate, derived from this background - proven by setting
  it to magenta and watching the ring turn pink. The hue it picks is not predictable (dark brown gave
  olive, beige gives pale green), so the plate cannot be matched to the ring exactly. What it can do
  is be light, so plate and ring read as one light shape rather than as a badge inside a badge.

  The foreground is a PNG because the source is raster art, at all five densities so nothing is
  upscaled, and scaled by a per-pixel test to the largest size at which no opaque pixel leaves the
  72dp safe circle - so no ear or paw is clipped by a round, squircle or teardrop mask.

  The monochrome layer is the same silhouette, flat. It is also why this stays an adaptive icon: a
  legacy PNG has nowhere to put one, and themed icons would fall back to a generic treatment.
-->
<adaptive-icon xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <background android:drawable="@color/ic_launcher_background" />
    <foreground android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_foreground" />
    <monochrome android:drawable="@mipmap/ic_launcher_monochrome" />
</adaptive-icon>
